.Dd $Mdocdate: August 1 2026$
.Dt OPENBAR 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm openbar
.Nd status bar for X11 window managers
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl 1
.Op Fl c Ar path
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a status bar designed for
.Xr cwm 1
and other X11 window managers.
It displays system information including CPU speed and temperature,
free memory, load average, battery status, public and private
IP addresses, and VPN connection status.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl 1
Run one update cycle and exit.
.It Fl c Ar path
Use the configuration file at
.Ar path
instead of the default.
.El
.Pp
Configuration is loaded from, in order:
.Bl -bullet
.It
the file passed with
.Fl c
.It
.Pa ~/.openbar.conf
if it exists
.It
.Pa /etc/openbar.conf
.El
.Sh PROCESS ARCHITECTURE
.Nm
runs as two processes on
.Ox :
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Main process
Reads configuration, manages the X11 window, and queries
kernel interfaces for CPU, memory, load, battery, and
network interface statistics.
.It Network worker
A child process forked before sandboxing.
It fetches public IPv4 and IPv6 addresses from
.Pa ifconfig.me
over HTTP and returns validated results to the main
process through a bounded
.Xr socketpair 2
protocol.
The worker has no access to X11, the configuration file,
battery or sensor devices, or any other parent resource.
.El
.Sh XRESOURCES
Font and colors can be customized with Xresources entries:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
openbar.font: fixed
openbar.foreground: black
openbar.background: white
.Ed
.Pp
Defaults are
.Dq fixed ,
.Dq black ,
and
.Dq white .
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.openbar.conf
Per-user configuration.
.It Pa /etc/openbar.conf
System-wide configuration.
.El
.Sh SECURITY
.Ss Sandboxing
Both processes use
.Xr pledge 2
and
.Xr unveil 2
on
.Ox
to limit permitted system calls and filesystem visibility.
.Ss Main process
Filesystem visibility is locked to:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
the configuration file (read-only)
.It
.Pa /tmp/.X11-unix
.Pq read-write, X11 socket
.It
.Pa /dev/apm
.Pq read-only, only if the device node exists
.El
.Pp
The steady-state
.Xr pledge 2
set is derived from the enabled configuration options:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Dq stdio unix
Always required for basic I/O and X11.
.It Dq sysctl
Added when
.Ar cpu
or
.Ar mem
is enabled.
.It Dq rpath ioctl
Added when
.Ar bat
is enabled
.Po access to
.Pa /dev/apm
.Pc .
.It Dq route
Added when
.Ar vpn
or
.Ar net
is enabled
.Po
.Xr getifaddrs 3
.Pc .
.El
.Pp
The init pledge
.Dq stdio rpath unix
is reduced to the steady-state set after reading the
configuration file and creating the X11 window.
.Nm
never retains
.Dq inet ,
.Dq dns ,
or
.Dq exec
in the main process.
.Ss Network worker
The worker runs under
.Dq stdio inet dns
with filesystem visibility locked to
.Pa /etc/hosts ,
.Pa /etc/resolv.conf ,
and
.Pa /etc/services
.Pq all read-only .
It has no access to the X11 display, system configuration,
battery device, or any kernel interface.
A compromised worker cannot reach the main process memory,
spawn processes, or access the filesystem beyond DNS
resolution helpers.
.Ss IPC protocol
Communication between parent and child uses a fixed-size
message structure over
.Xr socketpair 2 .
The parent sends a one-byte fetch command; the child
responds with a bounded 112-byte response containing
status codes and the address strings.
Unknown message types are ignored; truncated or oversized
responses cause the parent to fall back to
.Dq N/A .
.Ss Descriptor isolation
The child closes its standard input, standard output, and
the parent's socketpair endpoint immediately after
.Xr fork 2 .
The parent closes the child's socketpair endpoint.
No file descriptors cross the
.Xr exec 3
boundary
.Pq the child never calls exec .
.Ss Privilege dropping
.Nm
runs as the invoking user; no setuid, privilege escalation,
or
.Xr chroot 2
is performed.
All sandbox restrictions are applied in the child before
any network I/O, and in the parent before the main event
loop.
.Ss Limitations
All
.Xr pledge 2
promises and
.Xr unveil 2
paths documented above require runtime verification
through
.Xr ktrace 1 ,
regression tests, and realistic workloads on a real
.Ox
system.
None of the policies have been verified through execution tracing.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cwm 1 ,
.Xr pledge 2 ,
.Xr unveil 2 ,
.Xr openbar.conf 5
